{
	"_comment": "Per-TOKEN USD prices (LiteLLM field names). Multiply by 1e6 for per-MTok. Dated model ids (e.g. claude-opus-4-5-20251101) fall back to the dateless alias by stripping a trailing -YYYYMMDD. cache_creation_input_token_cost = 5-minute cache write (1.25x input); *_above_1hr = 1-hour cache write (2x input); cache_read = 0.1x input, EXCEPT Fable 5.1 and Mythos 5.1 which bill cache hits at 0.025x input ($0.25/MTok), and Opus 5.5 at 0.05x input ($0.20/MTok). *_above_200k_tokens = the >200K-context surcharge tier (Sonnet 4.5 only; other current models are flat to 1M). Source: _docs/04-pricing-and-limits.md.",
	"_comment_openai": "gpt-*/gpt-*-codex are OpenAI API LIST prices (cached input = 0.1x input). GPT-6 Astra and GPT-5.6 explicitly bill every cache write at 1.25x input, so both normalized write fields carry that rate; older entries use calculator fallbacks. GPT-6 Astra launched at 10/50. GPT-5.6 Luna and Terra were cut on 2026-07-30 (Luna 1/6 -> 0.20/1.20, Terra 2.50/15 -> 2/12); Sol promotional pricing later cut 5/30 -> 4/20. For a Codex ChatGPT subscription (plan_type=pro) these produce a NOTIONAL 'what it would cost at API rates' figure, not real spend \u2014 always label it as such. Rates verified from official OpenAI pricing (2026). Intentionally FLAT (no *_above_200k fields): OpenAI's long-context cliff is not CostCalculator's fixed 200K threshold, so adding above_200k fields here would misfire \u2014 generalize the threshold before ever adding an OpenAI context tier.",
	"_comment_gemini": "gemini-* are Google Gemini API LIST prices (per-MTok: 2.5-pro 1.25/10, 2.5-flash 0.30/2.50, 2.5-flash-lite 0.10/0.40, 3-pro 2/12, 3-flash 0.50/3; cached input ~0.1x). Gemini CLI on OAuth / Gemini Code Assist is a subscription, so these produce a NOTIONAL figure, never real spend \u2014 always label it as such. Pro tiers carry the >200K-context surcharge (2.5-pro 2.5/15, 3-pro 4/18): Gemini's cliff IS at 200K, matching CostCalculator's threshold, so *_above_200k fields are correct here (unlike OpenAI's 400K). Flash tiers are flat. Gemini exposes no cache-creation split, so no cache_creation fields. Verified against ai.google.dev/pricing (2026).",
	"_comment_grok": "grok-* are xAI API LIST prices from https://docs.x.ai/developers/pricing (per-MTok). Official cached-input rates are model-specific (NOT a flat 0.1x): grok-4.7 and grok-4.6 2.00/0.50/6.00, grok-4.5 2.00/0.30/6.00, grok-4.3 and 4.20 family 1.25/0.20/2.50, grok-build-0.1 code API 1.00/0.20/2.00. Grok Build CLI on SuperGrok / X Premium+ is a subscription, so these produce a NOTIONAL figure, never real spend \u2014 always label it as such. Aliases: locally emitted grok-4.7-build \u2192 grok-4.7 (xAI documents no grok-4.7 alias); locally emitted grok-4.6-build plus official grok-4.6-latest \u2192 grok-4.6; locally emitted grok-4.5-build plus official grok-4.5-latest/grok-build-latest/grok-build \u2192 grok-4.5; grok-4.3-latest/grok-latest \u2192 grok-4.3; grok-code-fast* \u2192 grok-build-0.1; common grok-4.20-* aliases \u2192 grok-4.20-0309-reasoning. No cache-creation split. xAI applies a 200K-prompt premium (2x input/cached/output for grok-4.7, grok-4.6 and grok-4.5, 2x for the 4.3/4.20/build family) billed across the WHOLE request; that threshold is exactly CostCalculator's 200000 tier, so *_above_200k fields are correct here (unlike OpenAI's 272K). grok-composer-* are Cursor rates with no published cliff, so they carry no tier. Source: docs.x.ai/developers/pricing + per-model pages.",
	"_comment_grok_composer": "grok-composer-* are Cursor Composer 2.5 on-demand rates from https://cursor.com/changelog/composer-2-5 (Fast default $3/$15; Standard $0.50/$2.50 per MTok). Grok Build emits these IDs in updates.jsonl modelUsage when routing to Cursor's agent \u2014 still NOTIONAL against the subscription/credit pool, never real API spend. Cursor publishes no cached-input rate; omit cache_read here and let CostCalculator fall back to 0.1\u00d7 input.",
	"_comment_opencode": "OpenCode-family rows. deepseek-v4-flash, deepseek-v4-flash-vision-exp, deepseek-v4-pro, muse-spark-1.2 and muse-spark-1.3 are OpenCode Zen gateway LIST prices from https://opencode.ai/docs/zen/ (per-MTok: V4 Flash 0.14/0.28/cached 0.028, V4 Pro 1.74/3.48/cached 0.145, Muse Spark 1.25/4.25/cached 0.15); the Zen page is the operative list for ids JP only ever sees through OpenCode. deepseek-v4.1-flash is not on the Zen page (OpenCode Go emits it) and carries DeepSeek's own OFF-PEAK API rate from https://api-docs.deepseek.com/quick_start/pricing (0.15/0.60/cached 0.003; peak is 2x). deepseek-v4-flash-go, deepseek-v4-flash-vision-exp-go and deepseek-v4-pro-go are Go LIST prices from https://opencode.ai/docs/go/ (per-MTok: V4 Pro 0.66/1.98/cached 0.022, V4 Flash 0.15/0.60/cached 0.003) and are reached ONLY by parse-time `-go` suffixing of Go-routed turns (OpenCodeGateway) — the base ids stay Zen prices. OpenCode Go models (glm-5.x, kimi-k2.x, qwen3.x, minimax-m2.x/m3, mimo-v2.5, longcat-2.0, hy3, hy4-preview, union-alpha) use the published per-token rates from https://opencode.ai/docs/go/ (per-MTok as listed). big-pickle and union-alpha are OpenCode's free house models (0/0). FREE-TIER ids (`-free`, `-contributor-free`) are deliberately UNPRICED on every row \u2014 gateway and model-brand alike \u2014 never mapped to a paid base row (PricingTable.isFreeTier). mimo-v2.5 and nemotron-3-ultra have NO row on Zen (Zen serves both only as free tiers), but mimo-v2.5 is priced on Go so it carries a Go rate here. muse-spark-1.3-contributor and muse-spark-1.2-contributor are the Meta contributor-tier rates served through Go (0.10/0.20/cached 0.002), distinct from the non-contributor Zen/Meta rates. Legacy aliases muse-spark / meta-muse-spark-1.1 / meta/muse-spark-1.1 normalize to muse-spark-1.1 (PricingTable.normalizedModelID).",
